  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nModel ensembles indicate a ridge will build slowly eastward from\nthe Central CONUS through mid week. The increasing 500mb\nheights, and srly flow, will begin the increase in heat and\nhumidity later today. The continued rising 500mb heights will\ncontinue through mid week, with increasing temperatures each\nday. \n\nThe hottest days are expected to be Tuesday and Wednesday,\nwhen highs reach the upper 80s to lower 90s across much of the\narea outside of the higher terrain. Heat indices are expected \nto peak in the mid to upper 90s on Wednesday, with a few \nreadings near 100 possible. These higher readings will be highly\ndependent on the strength of the ridge, and any convection that\ncould limit surface heating. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nClouds will increase today ahead of an approaching shortwave.\nThis wave is progged to track southeast across the Lower Great\nLakes region tonight into Monday on the northeast periphery of \nthe ridge, with shower and possible thunderstorm chances north \nof Pittsburgh. Another wave is possible late Tuesday, resulting\nin shower and thunderstorm chances mainly near and north of\nI-80. Extent and exact location of POPs will be dependent on \nthe strength and northern extent of the ridge. \n\nThe ridge is progged to retrograde to the west from mid to late\nweek, resulting in NW flow aloft across the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion. Embedded shortwaves in the flow should result in\nincreasing shower and thunderstorm chances area wide, \nespecially late Wednesday into Thursday. With instability and \nincreasing flow aloft increasing shear, severe thunderstorms \nwill be possible. Current ML guidance and SPC outlooks indicate \nthe late Wednesday through Thursday would be the greatest \npotential for strong to severe storms. Uncertainty remains in \nthe exact details of convection timing and placement, and \ndepends on the breakdown of the ridge.",
